{"name":"Cowboy Hat","hex":"#b27d50","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cowboy hat","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A broad-brimmed hat worn by cowboys, ranchers, and farmers, common in the western United States and Mexico."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cowboy_hat"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cowboy hat","blocks":[{"text":"The cowboy hat is a high-crowned, wide-brimmed hat best known as the defining piece of attire for the North American cowboy. Today it is worn by many people, and is particularly associated with ranch workers in the United States, Canada, Mexico, Central America and Brazil, station workers in Australia and New Zealand, with many country, regional Mexican and sertanejo music performers, and with participants in the North American rodeo circuit. It is recognized around the world as part of traditional Old West apparel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Felthat.jpg/330px-Felthat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cowboy_hat"},{"title":"Cowboy","blocks":[{"text":"A cowboy is an animal herder who tends cattle on ranches in North America, traditionally on horseback, and often performs many other ranch-related tasks. The historic American cowboy of the late 19th century arose from the vaquero traditions of northern Mexico, which trace their roots back to Spanish settlers from Andalusia and became a figure of special significance and legend. A subtype, called a wrangler, specifically tends the horses used to work cattle. In addition to ranch work, some cowboys work for or participate in rodeos. Cowgirls, first defined as such in the late 19th century, had a less-well documented historical role, but in the modern world work at identical tasks and have obtained considerable respect for their achievements. Cattle handlers in many other parts of the world, particularly South America and Australia, perform work similar to the cowboy.\nThe cowboy has deep historic roots tracing back to Spain and the earliest European settlers of the Americas. Over the centuries, differences in terrain and climate, and the influence of cattle-handling traditions from multiple cultures, created several distinct styles of equipment, clothing and animal handling. Equipment and techniques have adapted over time, though many classic traditions are preserved."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/HerdQuit.jpg/330px-HerdQuit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cowboy"},{"title":"Hat","blocks":[{"text":"A hat is a head covering which is worn for various reasons, including protection against weather conditions, ceremonial reasons such as university graduation, religious reasons, comedy, safety, or as a fashion accessory. Hats which incorporate mechanical features, such as visors, spikes, flaps, braces or beer holders shade into the broader category of headgear."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Chapeaux_en_peau_de_castor.jpg/330px-Chapeaux_en_peau_de_castor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hat"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Trajes_mexicanos_rancheros_-_Johan_Moritz_Rugendas_%281830%E2%80%99s%29.jpg/500px-Trajes_mexicanos_rancheros_-_Johan_Moritz_Rugendas_%281830%E2%80%99s%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Trajes_mexicanos_rancheros_-_Johan_Moritz_Rugendas_%281830%E2%80%99s%29.jpg/500px-Trajes_mexicanos_rancheros_-_Johan_Moritz_Rugendas_%281830%E2%80%99s%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typical Mexican sombrero of the 1830s to 1850s.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATrajes_mexicanos_rancheros_-_Johan_Moritz_Rugendas_(1830%E2%80%99s).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Oliver_Tree_en_entrevista_de_2022_%28cropped_2%29.png/500px-Oliver_Tree_en_entrevista_de_2022_%28cropped_2%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Oliver_Tree_en_entrevista_de_2022_%28cropped_2%29.png/500px-Oliver_Tree_en_entrevista_de_2022_%28cropped_2%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Oliver Tree, American songwriter wearing a cowboy hat","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOliver_Tree_en_entrevista_de_2022_(cropped_2).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/%C2%ABEl_Hacendado%C2%BB_por_Louis_Falconnet_%281865%29.jpg/500px-%C2%ABEl_Hacendado%C2%BB_por_Louis_Falconnet_%281865%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/%C2%ABEl_Hacendado%C2%BB_por_Louis_Falconnet_%281865%29.jpg/500px-%C2%ABEl_Hacendado%C2%BB_por_Louis_Falconnet_%281865%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typical Mexican charro hat of the 1850s and 1860s which may have influenced John B. Stetson","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%C2%ABEl_Hacendado%C2%BB_por_Louis_Falconnet_(1865).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/1800s_Boss_of_the_plains_5.jpg/330px-1800s_Boss_of_the_plains_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/1800s_Boss_of_the_plains_5.jpg/330px-1800s_Boss_of_the_plains_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Boss of the Plains hat","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1800s_Boss_of_the_plains_5.jpg